> In 1948, the Soviet Union unveiled a new long-range bomber, similar[ in] design and performance to the American B[-]29 bomber[.] The following year, the Soviets detonated its first nuclear weapon[.] By 1950, both the Canadian and American[ government] recognized that the main threat to North American security was a long-range Soviet[ bomber force.] Consequently, the two governments cooperated in the construction of three warning lines, networks of radar[ stations] positioned across North America.
The Pine Tree line was a series of radar[ installations] along the 49th parallel. The Mid-Canada line was built along the 55th parallel, and the DEW (Distant Early Warning) line stretched from Alaska to Greenland along the Arctic[ coast] of North America[. In] the event of a Soviet[ bomber] attack, these radar installations would provide Canadian and American[ air forces four] to six[ hours] warning. Both Royal Canadian[ Air] Force (RC[AF]) and United States Air[ Force] (USAF) fighters could[ be] deployed to intercept any Soviet[ bomber force].

